WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.451
Imagine two classrooms joined by one corridor. If the corridor is blocked, a message in one room cannot reach the other. Your brain is built like this. It has two halves, and each half is called a hemisphere. The corridor between them is the corpus callosum.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:37.297
Some people have severe epilepsy, which means sudden attacks of abnormal brain activity. To stop the attacks, surgeons once cut the corpus callosum. These people are called split-brain patients. Their two halves could no longer pass messages.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:56.982
The wiring crosses over. The left hand and the left side of what you see go to the right hemisphere. The right hand and the right side of vision go to the left hemisphere. The left hemisphere handles language, so it can speak. The right hemisphere cannot.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:14.648
Now the study. Sperry, nineteen sixty-eight, tested eleven split-brain patients. In one task they felt an object with one hand, out of sight. In another, a picture was flashed to one side of their vision. Each time, only one hemisphere received the information.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:22.104
A question describes a split-brain patient doing a task. You must say what the person can say or do, using Sperry.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:47.085
Step one: work out which hemisphere gets the information. Left hand or left vision means the right hemisphere. This comes first because everything else depends on it. Step two: recall what Sperry found for that side. Step three: say what the person can or cannot say or do, using details from the question.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:02:01.048
The trap is saying the patient cannot feel or find the object. They can find it. They just cannot name it. So write about speaking, not sensing.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:23.106
Here is a real exam question. Hina is a split-brain patient. She picks a marble from a bag using her left hand, without seeing it. She is then asked to tell the audience what she picked. We must explain what she is likely to say, using Sperry. It is worth two marks.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:34.325
Step one. Hina used her left hand. The left hand connects to the right hemisphere. So the marble is felt by the right hemisphere only.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:50.133
Step two. Sperry found that patients could not name objects felt with the left hand. The right hemisphere cannot speak, and the cut corpus callosum stops the information reaching the left hemisphere, which can.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:03:03.803
A tempting wrong route is to say Hina cannot tell what is in her hand because she cannot feel it. But she did feel it and pick it. Her problem is saying the word.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:18.052
Now the exam-ready answer. Sperry found that split-brain patients could not name objects felt with the left hand. So Hina probably cannot say marble, though she could point to it or pick it again.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:25.444
That is one mark for the finding and one for the application. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:39.648
A common mistake is to say the patient cannot find or sense the object. Sperry showed they can still pick it out. What they cannot do is say its name.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:57.304
Now you try. Amy shows split-brain patients a funny video, only to the right hemisphere. Describe what she is likely to find, using Sperry. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:17.232
The right hemisphere can process emotion. In Sperry's picture task, patients giggled or blushed at a shown picture. So the patients will probably laugh or smile at the dogs. But they cannot say what they saw, because the left hemisphere never received the video.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:31.963
A common wrong answer says the patients show no reaction because they cannot see the video. But the right hemisphere sees it fine. It just cannot speak. So that answer loses the mark.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:39.647
So find the side. Recall Sperry's finding. Predict what the person can say or do.
